<!-- §01 -->
<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Sequential Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Generating tokens one at a time is fundamentally slow</h2>
<p>Autoregressive LLMs have dominated NLP because they model sequences exactly: every token is conditioned on every token before it. The price is architectural: generating token <em>t</em> requires a full forward pass that reads the entire KV cache, then token <em>t+1</em> requires another pass, and so on. With a KV cache that can be hundreds of gigabytes, each step is a round-trip across memory bandwidth — not compute. The GPU sits idle waiting for data, not because the model is too big, but because sequential access patterns fundamentally waste the hardware.</p>

<p>Three solutions exist. First, diffusion language models (DLMs) generate all tokens in a block simultaneously — but they break the causal chain, producing drift. Second, speculative decoding (EAGLE, DFlash) trains a small draft model to generate a block of candidates which the full model then verifies in one pass — but this requires a separate KV cache, doubling memory. Third, Orthrus: inject a diffusion head <em>inside</em> the frozen AR model, reusing its KV cache exactly.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">generation_race.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Play to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="gen-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="gen-controls">
      <button id="gen-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button id="gen-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="pass-tag">forward passes: <span id="pass-display">—</span></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The canvas above shows generating 8 tokens from the prompt "3 + 5 =". Autoregressive decoding (top) fires one pass per token — 8 passes total. Orthrus (bottom) fires two passes: one parallel projection from the diffusion head, one validation from the AR head. Both produce identical outputs; Orthrus is just 4× faster for this block.</p>

<p>The key constraint Orthrus must satisfy: the final output must be drawn from the <em>exact same distribution</em> as the frozen AR baseline. Not approximately. Exactly. This is not a quality claim — it is a mathematical guarantee enforced by the consensus mechanism.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 -->
<section id="s-drift" data-code="drift">
<div class="section-no">02 — Why Diffusion Language Models Drift</div>
<h2>Parallel generation requires a conditional independence assumption that breaks reasoning</h2>

<p>To generate tokens in parallel, a diffusion language model must assume the tokens within a block are conditionally independent given the context. Formally, whereas AR decomposes the joint as a strict product of causal conditionals:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Autoregressive — exact chain rule</div>
  <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>AR</sub>(x) = ∏<sub>i=1..N</sub> <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">x<sub>i</sub></span> | <span class="math-var">x</span><sub>&lt;i</sub>)
  <div class="math-comment" style="margin-top:.4rem">— token i strictly conditioned on all prior tokens</div>
</div>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Diffusion LM — conditional independence approximation</div>
  <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>DLM</sub>(y | c) ≈ ∏<sub>k∈M</sub> <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>φ</sub>(<span class="math-var">y<sub>k</sub></span> | c, <span class="math-var">ỹ</span>)
  <div class="math-comment" style="margin-top:.4rem">— each masked token predicted independently given corrupted block ỹ</div>
</div>

<p>This approximation is catastrophic for long-horizon reasoning. Consider generating the chain "since x²=4, x=2, so x+1=3". Every token is logically conditioned on its predecessors. A DLM predicting "x+1=___" in parallel with "x²=4" has no access to the intermediate steps it hasn't "officially" decided yet — it must treat them as uncertain. Errors compound across positions.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>The 11-point drop.</strong> Fast-dLLM-v2, a state-of-the-art AR→DLM adaptation using 500B tokens of continual training, drops 11.1 points on MATH-500 relative to its AR baseline. Orthrus drops zero — because it never modifies the AR head.
</div>

<p>The fundamental problem with existing adaptations is that they fine-tune the AR weights to optimize a diffusion objective. Once the weights change, you can never recover the original AR distribution. Orthrus avoids this entirely by keeping the AR backbone frozen and training only the new diffusion head.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Orthrus Architecture</div>
<h2>A frozen AR head builds the context; a trainable diffusion head generates in parallel</h2>

<p>Every transformer layer in Orthrus has two parallel attention paths sharing a single KV cache. The frozen AR path (blue) uses standard causal self-attention — unchanged from the original pre-trained model. The trainable diffusion path (red) adds new projection matrices initialized from the AR weights, but updated during fine-tuning to perform bidirectional block attention over masked positions.</p>

