<p>The key structural insight is the <strong>shared KV cache</strong>. In standard speculative decoding, the draft model and the verifier are separate networks, each needing its own KV cache. Storing two caches for a 32K-context 8B model requires roughly 2× the memory. Orthrus eliminates this because the diffusion head lives inside the AR transformer — it naturally reuses the same K and V tensors.</p>

<h3>Training the diffusion head</h3>
<p>Since the AR backbone is frozen, training is pure distillation: align the diffusion head's parallel predictions with the AR model's exact output distribution. The loss is forward KL over masked block positions:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Orthrus training objective</div>
  ℒ = 𝔼 [ Σ<sub>b,k</sub> D<sub>KL</sub>( <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>AR</sub>(· | x<sub>≤aₙ+k-1</sub>) ‖ <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>diff</sub>(· | x<sub>&lt;aₙ</sub>, ỹ<sub>b</sub>) ) ]
  <div class="math-comment" style="margin-top:.4rem">— distill the full AR predictive distribution into the parallel diffusion head</div>
</div>

<p>Only the diffusion head's three projection matrices (Q, K, V) are updated. Total trainable parameters: ~16% of the model. Training: 600K examples, &lt;24 hours on a single 8×H200 node — orders of magnitude cheaper than pre-training a DLM from scratch.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 -->
<section id="s-inference" data-code="inference">
<div class="section-no">04 — Inference: Project, Validate, Advance</div>
<h2>Two passes, K tokens: one parallel projection, one exact validation</h2>

<p>The Orthrus inference loop is a continuous cycle. At each iteration, the diffusion head projects K candidate tokens in a single forward pass, then the frozen AR head validates them. Accepted tokens are committed to the sequence and KV cache; the first rejection triggers a resampled correction from AR. The loop repeats from the new anchor.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">inference_loop.steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to walk through</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="step-list">
      <div class="step-box" id="step-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Prefill — AR head builds KV cache</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The frozen AR backbone processes the full prompt "2 × 6 = 12, so 2 × 7 =" in one parallel forward pass, producing causal KV representations for every prompt token. This is identical to standard AR prefilling — no change from baseline.</div>
          <div class="step-code">cache ← AR_backbone.prefill(prompt)<br>anchor ← AR_head.greedy(cache)  # first output token</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Project — diffusion head generates K candidates in one pass</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The diffusion head takes the anchor token ("14") concatenated with K-1 mask tokens [M, M, M, …] and processes them jointly against the frozen KV cache. Bidirectional attention across the mask block lets every position attend to every other, yielding K simultaneous predictions.</div>
          <div class="step-code">block = [anchor] + [MASK] * (K-1)   # K=32<br>candidates = diff_head(block, cache)  # one pass → 32 tokens</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Consensus — AR validates left-to-right, commits accepted tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The AR head re-scores all K candidates in one pass. For greedy decoding, a token is accepted iff it matches the AR greedy choice. For sampling, exact rejection sampling aligns probabilities. Validation halts at the first rejection; the correction is drawn from AR, then the loop continues.</div>
          <div class="consensus-row" id="consensus-row">
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="0">14</span>
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="1">,</span>
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="2">and</span>
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="3">2</span>
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="4">×</span>
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="5">8</span>
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="6">is</span>
            <span class="tok-chip" data-idx="7">wrong</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-code">for k in range(K):<br>  if candidates[k] == AR_greedy(cache, accepted_so_far):<br>    accept(candidates[k])  # append to output + cache<br>  else:<br>    resample_from_AR(); break  # lossless correction</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
      <span id="step-prog">Step 1 of 3</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The consensus step is structurally identical to speculative decoding's accept/reject — except the "drafter" is the internal diffusion head and the "verifier" is the frozen AR head within the same model. Because both reside in the same transformer and share the same KV cache, there is no communication overhead between them and no memory duplication.</p>

<p>The critical theorem: because the acceptance criterion uses exact rejection sampling (Leviathan et al., 2022), the distribution of accepted tokens is provably equal to the AR distribution, regardless of how good or bad the diffusion head's proposals are. The diffusion head improves throughput; the consensus mechanism guarantees correctness.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 -->
<section id="s-blocksize" data-code="blocksize">
<div class="section-no">05 — Block Size K: The Free Throughput Knob</div>
<h2>Larger K = more tokens per pass, with constant forward-pass latency</h2>

<p>K is the number of positions in the parallel block — including the anchor token and K-1 mask tokens. Because the diffusion head processes all K positions simultaneously against a pre-computed KV cache, the forward-pass latency is essentially independent of K. Doubling K costs nothing in latency but can double the tokens committed per cycle.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">block_size_ablation.canvas — MATH-500 on Qwen3-8B</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a K value</span>
  </div>
