<p>The chart shows Tokens Per Forward Pass (TPF, purple) and forward-pass latency in milliseconds (amber) across K values on MATH-500. TPF scales from 1.85 at K=4 to 6.35 at K=32 — a 3.4× improvement — while latency stays essentially flat (~40ms). This is the key insight: the diffusion head is compute-bound on the attention to the KV cache, not on the number of mask tokens it outputs.</p>

<p>Orthrus sets K=32 for all reported results. Larger K is possible in principle but the acceptance rate curves off — once the diffusion head's projection quality saturates, additional mask positions contribute diminishing tokens per cycle.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Exact AR accuracy at 6× the speed — no other method achieves both</h2>

<p>Orthrus is evaluated on Qwen3-8B across eight benchmarks spanning math, code, and logic. The key headline: on every single benchmark, Orthrus matches the frozen AR baseline exactly (MATH-500: 86.2%, GSM8K: 96.0%, AIME24: 28.3%, HumanEval: 95.1%) while running at 5×–7.8× the speed. No other parallel decoding approach comes close on both axes simultaneously.</p>

<h3>MATH-500 accuracy vs competing diffusion approaches</h3>
