<p>The acceptance length comparison is striking: EAGLE-3, a purpose-built speculative drafter, achieves 3.5 on MATH-500. DFlash gets 7.9. Orthrus reaches 11.7 — nearly 3× EAGLE-3 — despite requiring no external draft model and consuming zero additional KV cache memory. The shared-context advantage is real: the diffusion head attends directly to the same high-fidelity representations the AR head uses, rather than relying on a compressed approximation in a smaller drafter.</p>

<h3>Long-context stability</h3>
<p>At 40K context length, DFlash throughput drops to ~43 tokens/sec as dual KV caches dominate memory bandwidth. Orthrus sustains ~96 tokens/sec across all context lengths from 16K to 40K. The zero-overhead shared cache is not just a memory budget advantage — it eliminates the bandwidth scaling problem entirely.</p>
